Sealed ARM64 image, network none, GCC 14.2.0-19:
2c23684c9cc5ef5899073b59844fa47cc3c1d894f2316e21f25d5d85be5a10e2
Built upstream Linux-x86_64-GCC makefiles with the ARM64 compiler: their
platform headers use only little-endian layout and generic GCC integer
builtins, not x86 instructions. SoftFloat SPECIALIZE_TYPE=ARM-VFPv2;
TestFloat TESTFLOAT_OPTS=-DFLOAT64; no LONG_DOUBLE_IS_EXTFLOAT80 assumption.

Each stream is testfloat_gen -seed 1 -level 1 -rnear_even OP, except FP-to-int
uses -rminMag. The entire stream passes through the production engine adapter
crates/evocc-types/examples/testfloat_values.rs; upstream and adapter exit 0.
No generated corpus is stored, sampled, prefiltered or rounded for comparison.
NaN payloads and signed zero compare exactly. Invalid integer conversions
must return no C value, not SoftFloat's saturated/fallback integer result.

Operation(s)                       Cases each   Invalid integer cases each
f32_add/sub/mul/div/eq/lt/le         46,464       0
f64_add/sub/mul/div/eq/lt/le         46,464       0
f32_to_f64                         600          0
f64_to_f32                         768          0
i32/ui32_to_f32/f64                372          0
i64/ui64_to_f32/f64                756          0
f32_to_i32/ui32/i64/ui64            600          177 / 276 / 97 / 237
f64_to_i32/ui32/i64/ui64            768          272 / 357 / 170 / 312
Total: 32 operations, 661,848 cases, zero differences;
1,898 invalid integer conversions explicitly rejected, not skipped.

The freshly built SoftFloat reference also passes upstream testsoftfloat
against its independent slowfloat implementation for these operation families
at level 1, seed 1 and the corresponding rounding modes.
Adapter negative checks detect a one-bit changed result, a changed conversion
domain flag, malformed fields and empty input (which cannot yield PASS).

This checks baseline numerical values and the C FP-to-int domain, not strict
exception flags, all rounding modes, decimal parsing, 128-bit integer APIs,
binary128 execution, libm accuracy or native public ABI. Those have distinct
existing tests and boundaries; no IEEE-completeness claim follows.
